From f8ae3f10d4204536bddd08f878e6b891f23a914d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Andre Watson <dre@ligandal.com>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 16:39:10 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] auxiliary work yields to the interactive lanes instead of
 competing with them
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

An agent framework makes two kinds of request to the same backend: the turn a
human is waiting on, and auxiliary work -- titles, compression, a goal judge,
a background review. On a dedicated endpoint the distinction does not matter.
On a shared one it decides whether the interactive lane keeps its radix prefix
cached or re-prefills it from scratch.

Measured over 24 h on one profile: 6,069 requests to the shared backend, of
which 682 (11.2%) were auxiliary. The interactive lanes spent 37.62M prompt
tokens on radix-eviction re-prefill -- 8,549 s of prefill emitting no output
token -- in the gaps where an auxiliary request was in flight, against 7.34M
where none was. That is 143,596 versus 5,028 wasted tokens per call, so 83.7%
of the mains' re-prefill waste is auxiliary-overlapped.  [measured]

Two things the design comment in the auxiliary client assumed, and which were
not true here:

 1. The profile pinned all 20 auxiliary tasks to the main model and base_url
    byte-identically, so the "route auxiliary work to another card" branch
    short-circuited on main_model == model and never looked. The other-card
    route was never expressed in configuration.
 2. The largest auxiliary consumer never reached the auxiliary client at all.
    A monitor loop shells out to a full one-shot CLI session every 240 s: 305
    calls carrying 12.9M prompt tokens (mean 42,340 -- a fresh system prompt
    per tick) for 222K output tokens, a 58:1 prefill-to-decode ratio. The CLI
    classified that turn as INTERACTIVE, so it ran at the same request
    priority as a turn a person typed.  [measured]

Changes:

  * the turn-priority decision is extracted into turn_request_priority(), and
    a one-shot -q/-Q run is classified NORMAL rather than INTERACTIVE. The
    file already asserted the fact this rests on, where it sets
    HERMES_SINGLE_QUERY_SESSION: a -q run has no user waiting.
  * background auxiliary tasks carry an explicit low priority, stamped through
    the broker's documented transport hook rather than by editing the broker.
    It is an ALLOW-list, not a deny-list: most auxiliary work sits ON the
    turn's critical path, and demoting that would slow the lane it is meant to
    protect. The goal judge is never demoted -- it rides the control-plane
    lane at top priority.
  * judge-shaped auxiliary prompts are bounded at _build_call_kwargs, the one
    choke point every retry and fallback route rebuilds through. compression,
    vision, web_extract, skills_hub, mcp, approval and the mixture-of-agents
    tasks are exempt: their input IS the payload, and truncating compression
    would silently discard the transcript the summary exists to preserve.
  * a one-shot session keeps its instantly derived title and skips the model
    call -- 258 calls a day on this profile, one per monitor tick, every one
    naming a session no human will ever list.
  * the background-review fork demotes itself. It replays a finished turn
    through a full agent loop, so its requests leave on the main client path
    where the auxiliary tag cannot reach them (1,716 calls, 55.4M prompt
    tokens).  [measured]

Squashed with the follow-up that fixes what the title change broke: returning
early left colliding one-shot sessions nameless, because the instant-title
write passes dedupe=False and writes nothing when the derived name is already
taken -- and on a machine lane it is taken every time (the suffix counter had
reached #905). The background stage is the only thing that picks a collision
back up with a lineage scan, so the stage still runs, with skip_llm=True: same
persistence, same collision recovery, no backend request.

Squashed from fork commits f8ae3f1, 1b84f9e.

BASE: the tree left by patch 0004 (see BASELINE.md).

PUBLISHED FORM. A private backend address becomes 127.0.0.1 and an absolute
fleet log path becomes <stack>/..., following the placeholder conventions in
NOTICE and ops/README.md.

